.TH tapectl-volume-abort 1  "tapectl-volume-abort " 
.SH NAME
tapectl\-volume\-abort \- Deliberately abandon a volume\*(Aqs unfinished write session (issue #94): `docs/design/layout\-session.md`\*(Aqs Aborted row, first clause. Use this when a `volume resume` reports a revalidation failure you know to be permanent (the staged data is really gone), or to clear a `planned` session that was killed before anything was written. Nothing can tell a transient cause from a permanent one but you, which is why resume never decides this on its own
.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBtapectl volume abort\fR [\fB\-\-json\fR] [\fB\-\-yes\fR] [\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR] [\fB\-v\fR|\fB\-\-verbose\fR] [\fB\-\-config\fR] [\fB\-h\fR|\fB\-\-help\fR] <\fILABEL\fR> 
.SH DESCRIPTION
Deliberately abandon a volume\*(Aqs unfinished write session (issue #94): `docs/design/layout\-session.md`\*(Aqs Aborted row, first clause. Use this when a `volume resume` reports a revalidation failure you know to be permanent (the staged data is really gone), or to clear a `planned` session that was killed before anything was written. Nothing can tell a transient cause from a permanent one but you, which is why resume never decides this on its own.
.PP
The tape is never contacted (hence no \-\-device): the cartridge is left unsealed and physically unharmed, and the staged files stay pinned until `staging clean` runs. The session, however, becomes unresumable for good.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
\fB\-\-json\fR
Output in JSON format
.TP
\fB\-\-yes\fR
Skip the confirmation prompt (ADR\-0008 Tier 2). Required in a non\-interactive session, which otherwise refuses rather than assuming consent
.TP
\fB\-\-dry\-run\fR
Show what would be done without making changes
.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Enable verbose output
.TP
\fB\-\-config\fR \fI<CONFIG>\fR
Path to config file
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help (see a summary with \*(Aq\-h\*(Aq)
.TP
<\fILABEL\fR>
Volume label
